The Security Council,
Recalling its previous resolutions and statements of its President concerning the situation in the Sudan and South Sudan, in particular resolutions 2729 (2024) and 2778 (2025),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of South Sudan, and the principles of good-neighbourliness and non-interference,
Reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Government of South Sudan to protect civilians and to ensure safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access,
Expressing grave concern at the continued violence and instability in South Sudan, including clashes in Upper Nile State and the attack on the hospital run by Médecins Sans Frontières in Old Fangak, Fangak County, on 3 May 2025, and at all attacks on civilians and civilian objects,
Deploring the humanitarian crisis, including displacement, food insecurity and obstruction of humanitarian assistance, and stressing the need for accountability for violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law,
Expressing grave concern at the impact of the conflict in Sudan on the stability and humanitarian situation in South Sudan, including cross-border displacement and reports of arms flows, and calling for respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of South Sudan and the Sudan,
Recognizing the important role of the United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS) in protecting civilians, facilitating humanitarian access, monitoring human rights, and supporting the implementation of the Revitalised Agreement,
Emphasizing the need for an inclusive political dialogue and credible preparations for elections, with the full and meaningful participation of women and youth,
Recognizing the important role of the African Union and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development in supporting peace and stability in South Sudan,
Determining that the situation in South Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS), as set out in resolution 2729 (2024), until 9 May 2026, and authorizes UNMISS to use all necessary means to carry out its tasks;
2. Decides that the mandate of UNMISS shall include, within existing resources and in line with resolution 2729 (2024), the following tasks:
(a) to protect civilians under threat of physical violence, including through proactive deployment and robust action;
(b) to facilitate safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access and to protect humanitarian personnel and facilities;
(c) to monitor, investigate and report on human rights violations and abuses, including conflict-related sexual violence, and to support accountability efforts;
(d) to support the implementation of the Revitalised Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in South Sudan, including security arrangements and ceasefire mechanisms;
(e) to support inclusive political dialogue, constitutional processes and preparations for free, fair and credible elections;
(f) to exercise good offices and support reconciliation and peacebuilding;
3. Condemns in the strongest terms the airstrike on the hospital run by Médecins Sans Frontières in Old Fangak, Fangak County, on 3 May 2025, and all attacks on civilians and civilian objects, including medical facilities and personnel; demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities and respect international humanitarian law and human rights law; and calls upon the Government of South Sudan to conduct a prompt, transparent and independent investigation and to hold those responsible accountable;
4. Demands that all parties, including armed groups, allow and facilitate full, safe, rapid and unhindered access for UNMISS and humanitarian actors to all areas of South Sudan, and ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel, humanitarian workers and medical personnel;
5. Calls upon the Government of South Sudan and all parties to implement the Revitalised Agreement, including security arrangements, and to engage in inclusive dialogue, with the full and meaningful participation of women and youth, to resolve political differences and prepare for credible elections;
6. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from any action that could undermine the peaceful resolution of the conflict, and to support regional efforts through the African Union and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development;
7. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Council at least every 90 days on the implementation of the UNMISS mandate, including on protection of civilians, humanitarian access, human rights and electoral preparations, and to include in his reports any violations of the Status of Forces Agreement;
8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

