Determining that the situation in South Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS), as set out in resolution 2729 (2024), until 9 May 2026, in full respect of the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of South Sudan, and with the consent of and in close consultation with the Government of South Sudan, and in accordance with the Status of Forces Agreement, and authorizes UNMISS to use all necessary means to carry out its tasks;
2. Decides that the mandate of UNMISS shall include, within existing resources and in line with resolution 2729 (2024), in full respect for the sovereignty and primary responsibility of the Government of South Sudan, and in close coordination with the Government of South Sudan, the African Union and IGAD, the following tasks:
(a) to protect civilians under threat of physical violence, including through proactive deployment and robust action, in close coordination with the Government of South Sudan and in accordance with the mandate;
(b) to facilitate safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access and to protect humanitarian personnel and facilities;
(c) to monitor, investigate and report on human rights violations and abuses, including conflict-related sexual violence and grave violations against children, and to support accountability efforts, including by supporting the collection and preservation of evidence of violations and abuses, efforts to strengthen the rule of law, and national accountability efforts;
(d) to support the implementation of the Revitalised Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in South Sudan, including security arrangements and ceasefire mechanisms;
(e) to support inclusive political dialogue, constitutional processes and preparations for free, fair and credible elections, with the full, equal and meaningful participation of women, youth and civil society, in a South Sudanese-led and South Sudanese-owned process, in accordance with the Revitalised Agreement and applicable national legal framework;
(f) to exercise good offices and support reconciliation and peacebuilding;
3. Condemns in the strongest terms the airstrike on the hospital run by Médecins Sans Frontières in Old Fangak, Fangak County, on 3 May 2025, and all attacks on civilians and civilian objects, including medical facilities and personnel; demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities and respect international humanitarian law and human rights law; calls for a prompt, transparent, independent and impartial investigation, including with the support of UNMISS and relevant United Nations and regional bodies where appropriate, and for those responsible to be held accountable; calls upon all parties to cooperate fully with that investigation, including by granting safe and unhindered access to the affected area and to medical personnel; and requests the Secretary-General to report on the outcome of the investigation through his reporting to the Council;
4. Demands that all parties, including armed groups, allow and facilitate full, safe, rapid and unhindered access for UNMISS and humanitarian actors to all areas of South Sudan, including Old Fangak, Fangak County, and the areas affected by recent clashes in Upper Nile State, and ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel, humanitarian workers and medical personnel; further demands that the Government of South Sudan fully respect the Status of Forces Agreement and immediately remove all restrictions on UNMISS freedom of movement, including at checkpoints and for flight clearances, in accordance with the Status of Forces Agreement;
5. Calls upon the Government of South Sudan and all parties to implement the Revitalised Agreement, including security arrangements, and to engage in inclusive dialogue, with the full, equal and meaningful participation of women, youth and civil society, to resolve political differences, prepare for credible elections, and create an enabling environment for civil society and political parties, including freedom of expression, association and assembly;
6. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from any action that could undermine the peaceful resolution of the conflict, including the transfer of arms and military materiel that fuel the conflict; to comply fully with the arms embargo concerning South Sudan; to take urgent measures to prevent the transfer of arms and ammunition that fuel the conflict; to respect the sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity of South Sudan and the Sudan; to refrain from external interference; and to support regional efforts through the African Union and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development;
7. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Council at least every 90 days on the implementation of the UNMISS mandate, including on protection of civilians, humanitarian access and obstacles thereto, human rights, electoral preparations, the full, equal and meaningful participation of women and youth, progress in implementing the Revitalised Agreement, arms flows and violations of relevant arms embargoes, cross-border displacement, the regional impact of the conflict in Sudan on South Sudan, any violations of the Status of Forces Agreement, and the investigation called for in paragraph 3, and, as appropriate, to consult with the African Union and IGAD in preparing those reports;
